Async Alembic migrations for {{ cookiecutter.project_name }}.

Alembic is the source of schema truth for every deployed environment. The local
environment may instead auto-create tables at startup (DB_AUTO_CREATE, on by
default only when ENVIRONMENT=local); staging and production must apply schema
with `alembic upgrade head`{% if cookiecutter.use_docker == "yes" %} — the compose api service runs it on boot{% endif %}.

Create a new revision after changing models:
    make revision m="describe your change"

Apply migrations:
    make migrate

Roll back the most recent migration:
    make downgrade

Generating revisions
--------------------
Autogenerate diffs the models against a live database, so it must run against
a database containing ONLY what earlier migrations created. Tables auto-created
by DB_AUTO_CREATE make autogenerate emit an empty revision. To generate the
baseline revision (versions/ ships empty) or any later one cleanly:
{% if cookiecutter.use_docker == "yes" %}
    docker compose down -v   # discard the auto-created local schema
    make db-up
    make migrate             # bring the fresh database up to head
    make revision m="baseline"
{%- else %}
    1. Point DATABASE_URL at an empty database.
    2. make migrate          # bring it up to head
    3. make revision m="baseline"
{%- endif %}

When you add a new domain, import its models module in migrations/env.py so
its tables register on the metadata autogenerate compares against.
{%- if cookiecutter.use_auth == "yes" %}

Note on users.email: AuthService lowercases emails on register/login, so
app-written rows arrive normalized. For defense in depth against out-of-band
writes, make the unique index case-insensitive in your first revision
(works on PostgreSQL and SQLite >= 3.9):
    op.create_index("ux_users_email_lower", "users", [sa.text("lower(email)")], unique=True)
{%- endif %}
